v3.2.24
Radon Player 3.2.24: Bit-perfect to USB DACs, fixed and steady
## 3.2.24: Bit-perfect to USB DACs, fixed and steady (Windows and Android)
- **Clean and explicit versions scan removed:** it didn't find clean songs reliably enough, so it's gone from Scanner. Your music › Clean or explicit (which version downloads get) and Most played counting both versions as one song stay as they were.
- **Bit-perfect to a USB DAC keeps playing:** with the DAC taken over, every song stopped a second in. Android (and some phones on their own) took the app taking the DAC for the DAC being unplugged and paused the music. That pause is now told apart from a real unplug and ignored, and unplugging the DAC while it plays still pauses. Anything that pauses the music just after the DAC is taken is noted in Problems, with why.
- **Wrapped shows your own songs:** a song in your top songs is now shown as your own copy of it, with its artist and its cover: Last.fm (or a service) can put plays under the wrong artist, and "What I Live For" by Juice WRLD showed as Lil Mboiz's, with their cover. A play matches your song when it's the same song, or when it names an artist you've no songs by and just one of your songs has that name; the plays of both are added up. Songs you haven't got show as your history has them.
- **Select and delete Discovery playlists:** in Discovery, hold a playlist (or tap Select) to pick it, tap more to pick them too, then Delete… (or Download, or Select all) acts on all of them at once, on the phone as on the PC.
- **The DJ with bit-perfect:** with the app streaming straight to a USB DAC, the DJ's voice wasn't heard at all (Android can't play anything through the DAC then) and the music didn't start again after the line. Now every DJ voice (ElevenLabs, the natural voice and the phone's own) is heard through the DAC, at the phone's volume, with the music held back under it, even before the first song (the DAC is set up for the voice), and the song starts when the DJ is done. The voice also went out muted: the music's volume goes to nothing while the DJ talks, and the stream to the DAC was muted with it; it now stays on for her. The next song isn't set up on the DAC until the DJ has finished (it used to replace or clear the stream mid-line), and the first lines with bit-perfect on each leave a note in Problems saying how they went to the DAC.
- **Visualizers with bit-perfect:** streaming straight to a USB DAC, the visualizers stood still: Android's visualizer only hears what goes through Android. They now read the music as the app sends it to the DAC, timed to what you're hearing.
- **Bit-perfect streams never pile up:** stopping one stream to the DAC while another part of the app started the next could leave the old one running beside the new one, unseen. The stream is now taken out before it's stopped, so there's only ever one.
- **Fewer false notes in Problems:** the DAC report said "music not getting through" when the music had only been paused (for the DJ, say). It's now noted only when the song really didn't move on while it played.
- **No more crashes after skipping many songs (or starting the DJ) with bit-perfect:** each time a song's stream to the DAC was closed, the app waited for only some of the transfers it had queued to come back, then freed their memory. After queuing more of them (to stop the split-second gaps), some always came back late, to the next song's stream, and the phone wrote them into memory already freed: sooner or later the app closed. Now every transfer is collected before a stream's memory is let go, and a stream only ever takes back its own.
- **Bit-perfect no longer goes on and off by itself:** after the app came back from a crash (or a song was tried again after an error), the music to a USB DAC went on and off over and over until you pressed Pause and Play. The DAC's stream could be started (or picked up again) paused while the player was playing, and stayed that way. It now always follows whether the player is playing.
- **A DAC that keeps disconnecting is noted:** when a USB DAC drops off the phone on its own twice within two minutes, Problems says so, with when: the phone lost it (the cable, the connector, or the DAC wanting more power than the phone gives), not the app.
- **Skipping songs quickly with bit-perfect no longer crashes the app:** skipping three songs in a row quickly while streaming straight to a USB DAC closed the app. A check on the DAC's stream (or a report) could still be reading a stream the player had just closed for the next song. Every look at a stream now first checks it's still open, and a closed one is simply skipped.
- **Bass and Treble with bit-perfect:** a new switch in the Equalizer (shown while bit-perfect is on) keeps the equalizer's Bass and Treble working with bit-perfect, on the phone and the PC: just those two, in full precision on the samples as they go to the DAC, turned down first by what they boost so nothing clips. The rest of the equalizer stays bypassed, and with both at 0 dB the music is untouched again.
- **Bit-perfect to a DAC without the split-second gaps:** when the DAC stopped taking music for a moment, the app took up to half a second to notice and the music paused for a split second. It now keeps 64 ms of music queued at the DAC (was 24), gives the stream audio priority as Android does its own, notices a stop within 0.15 seconds and sets the DAC up again straight away. A stream that stops on its own ("AudioTrack write failed: 32") is set up again too, rather than counted as a failed song.
- **Bit-perfect to a DAC no longer fills Problems:** once it worked, every song (and every pause) left a note in Problems: the reports added to find out why songs stopped. Now the DAC is only noted when something's really wrong (music not getting through, or one song's stream stopping again and again); getting going again after a pause, when the phone may sleep, is quiet.
- **Bit-perfect to a DAC no longer stops a second in:** the FiiO KA17's reports showed the phone's USB controller stopped sending it music about 1.6 seconds into every song, though the DAC was still there and answering. The app sent the music in 8 ms pieces; it now sends 1 ms ones, 24 ms ahead, as Linux's own USB audio driver does. And it watches the stream: if the DAC stops taking music, the transfers are sent again, and if that doesn't help, the DAC is set up again from scratch and the song carries on where it was (each noted in Problems).
- **No more "AudioTrack write failed: -1" with bit-perfect:** when another player (a preview, say) took the DAC, or its stream was stopped, the song playing failed and counted towards bit-perfect giving up. It now sets the DAC up again and carries on where it was, and a player that's let go or paused no longer stops, pauses or mutes another player's music on the DAC.
- **Volume buttons work with bit-perfect to a DAC:** the phone's volume buttons did nothing on the FiiO KA17: it accepted the app's volume setting but didn't get any quieter. Now the DAC's own volume stays at full and the phone's volume turns the music down in the app, as Android itself does for USB DACs (bit-perfect at full volume; songs go out in the DAC's deepest format, 32-bit on the KA17, so turning down loses nothing). The buttons also always set the music's volume while the app is open (they could change the ringer instead), and the volume is checked four times a second in case Android doesn't say it changed.
- **Bit-perfect to a USB DAC: no more static:** songs sent straight to a USB DAC (FiiO KA17 on a Samsung) came out as loud static. The app took only the DAC's streaming part from Android; Android's own USB audio driver kept the control part, so the app's settings for the DAC (its sample rate, its volume) were refused, and the DAC went on running as Android had set it. The app now takes the whole DAC over, as USB Audio Player Pro does, and checks the DAC really is in the format the music is packed for before playing (else that song plays through Android). Android then thinks the DAC was unplugged for a moment; the pause that caused is undone.
- **Bit-perfect off and on again works:** turned off and straight back on, bit-perfect to a DAC played static: the app took the DAC back while Android was still setting it up its own way. It now waits for Android to finish (a few seconds) and stops everything Android had the DAC doing before setting it up.
- **A song that fails over bit-perfect to a DAC is played again:** the DAC is handed back and the song tried once more; after two songs fail, bit-perfect plays through Android until you unplug the DAC and plug it in (or turn bit-perfect off and on), instead of every song stopping after a second. Each failure is noted in Problems with how the DAC was set up, to send.
- **Bit-perfect to a USB DAC, checked as it starts:** after setting the DAC to the song's sample rate, the app now reads the rate back. If the DAC didn't take it, which would make the music come out too fast or too slow (loud static or crackling), the song plays through Android instead, and Problems says so. The first streams of each session also leave a short report in Problems (what the DAC offered, what was picked, its rate, its feedback and any failed transfers), to send if something sounds wrong.
- **Volume buttons with bit-perfect to a DAC follow Android's curve:** half volume is now about -30 dB on the DAC (it was -15 dB), the same as the phone gives through Android, so the DAC isn't much louder than you expect when bit-perfect takes over.

### Good to know
- **Android:** install RadonPlayer-Next.apk over Radon Player Next (same app, same signature: your library, settings and stats stay), or tap Update in Settings.
- **Windows:** Radon-Player-3.2.24.msi installs over 3.2.23, or use Update in Settings.
